Fix a lost wakeup that deadlocks basisu's job_pool destructor.

The destructor set m_kill_flag and notified without holding m_mutex, which is the mutex a
worker evaluates its wait predicate under. A worker that read the predicate false and was
descheduled before enqueueing on the condition variable therefore missed the notify and
slept forever on a flag that was already true, and the destructor's join() never returned.
m_kill_flag being std::atomic removes the data race on the flag, not the lost wakeup.

Observed as an assetlib_tests shard wedged for 83 minutes at no CPU inside
ktxTexture2_CompressBasisEx, holding the machine-wide suite lock. See
docs/known_issues.md, "assetlib_tests hangs on one shard, at no CPU, inside a texture bake".

This is a backport: upstream basis_universal already takes the lock here, so the patch is
expected to stop applying when ktx next vendors a newer basisu -- which is the signal to
delete it and this overlay port with it.

--- a/external/basisu/encoder/basisu_enc.cpp
+++ b/external/basisu/encoder/basisu_enc.cpp
@@ -1532,10 +1532,16 @@
 	job_pool::~job_pool()
 	{
 		debug_printf("job_pool::~job_pool\n");
-		
-		// Notify all workers that they need to die right now.
-		m_kill_flag = true;
 		
+		// Notify all workers that they need to die right now. The flag is set under the mutex a
+		// worker evaluates its wait predicate under: without it, a worker that has read the
+		// predicate false but has not yet enqueued on the condition variable misses this notify
+		// and sleeps on a flag that is already true, and the join below never returns.
+		{
+			std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_mutex);
+			m_kill_flag = true;
+		}
+
 		m_has_work.notify_all();
 
 		// Wait for all workers to die.
